00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
my talk at the table is filmmaker  alexandra pelosi  you may recall her from the national  attention she gained  with her documentary journeys with  george showing president george w bush  during his 2000 campaign pelosi who  happens to be the daughter  of house speaker nancy pelosi went back  on the road  last year to film a new movie it's  called american selfie  one nation shoots itself a lot of  meaning in that title  it shows americans documenting social  unrest during the pandemic  and as you're about to see one young  woman spoke about the growing demand  for racial justice i was 10 years old  when trayvon martin was murdered  and i remember watching the case  optimistic just knowing in my heart  zimmerman would be  recharged and then she was acquitted of  all charges and  eight years later absolutely nothing has  changed  we've had hashtag after hashtag after  hashtag and absolutely nothing is  changing  so that is why i'm out here saying black  lives matter yet again which is  something we shouldn't even have to be  saying  in big old 2020 that we have to be  saying our lives matter  it's it's absolutely insane  old 2020. that says it all alexandra  pelosi joins us now  uh alexandra good morning to you first  uh congratulations on this film  i love it i think sound from everyday  people is so powerful  and what i want to make clear for people  is it's called american selfie but  this is not found footage you were out  there doing the work talking to people  getting interviews like the one we just  heard  and about that interview that's a young  woman talking about  10 years of change you've been doing  this for 20 odd years you've seen a lot  of political change  what is the difference between america  of the george bush years and america  today  and what caused that change  well i should mention that that was the  day  that the united states military used  chemical agents to shoot  at rubber bullets and chemical weapons  at  peaceful protesters that young lady is  an example of who was there that day  when the government turned on peaceful  protesters  before the curfew in america i you know  i've been doing this a long time  i was a network news producer for a  decade i've been making documentaries  this is my 13th documentary  but i never thought in my lifetime that  i would see my own government  turn on its own people and to this day i  still haven't quite gotten over the fact  that i was there watching the national  guard  shoot tear gas at innocent protesters in  peace time  in america i mean you expect that from  third world countries it's very  venezuela  but this is not america and that's  something that i think  people need to think about hold the  mirror up to this country at this moment  in time and say  we're better than this america  there's a moment in the film i believe  you're in las vegas you're talking to a  young man and you ask him is america  broken  and he says yes do you agree is america  broken  and and who broke her i would blame  everything i put it on the lap of social  media i mean i think that regardless of  who you're going to vote for in this  election  the one thing every single person i talk  to all across america  for a year everyone the one thing  everyone could agree on is  social media is doing real damage to our  democracy  it's completely undermining the  conversation it's making us hate each  other  it's feeding everyone these toxic feeds  that are just  you know it's capitalizing on the divide  and i feel like  leading up to the election everyone  should just turn their phones off  because it's doing  so much damage to our mental health  yeah what what makes the film so  powerful is it toggles between  vastly different realities in this  country and you get incredible authentic  sound from people  who are more fact-based than others and  seeing the transition between the two  i mean it must have been neck breaking  for you as a reporter as a producer as a  filmmaker  what was it like shuttling between these  two worlds  well you know this is my 13th film i've  been doing this for a very long time  and i think that what what struck me  this time around  is how the the america's like a raw open  wound  you know you walk out there and you talk  to people people are really decent  americans are good nice people but the  way it looks on cable news is that we're  all just  it's toxic and i feel like when you go  out and you talk to people you don't get  that impression there are lots of  nice people you can meet everywhere  across america i think that  people just need to take a step back  what i'm trying to do is this is like an  artifact  i'm trying to hold up the mirror and say  to america like this is how we look in  2020. this is our selfie  and we all just need to sort of take  take a deep breath  and unplug from our social toxic social  media feeds  and try to remember that we you know  there is a possibility we can get all  we all can get along after this election  we can't there is hope  for us boy i hope so i hope we take a  long look in the mirror and decide we  don't like our outfit we make a change  uh alexandra pelosi thank you very much  it's a powerful powerful film the  documentary is called american selfie  one nation shoots itself  it debuts tonight at 9 pm eastern and  pacific  on showtime that is a division of viacom  cbs

